NAME
====

**wattle-dependencies** - dependencies of Wattle projects

DESCRIPTION
===========

A *dependency* is code from another project that a Wattle project uses.
A project may include a copy of that code in its source tree or declare a
remote library in its info file. A copy included in the source tree is
*vendored* and committed with the project. Remote libraries are fetched into
`.wattle/` and are not committed.

Installing an application does not make its modules available to other
projects. Dependencies are obtained for each project. See
`wattle-projects(7)` for project layout and artifact declarations.

VENDORING
=========

*Vendoring* is the practice of copying a dependency into the project's
source tree. The copy becomes part of the project and may be edited by its
author. Running the project does not require the vendored source to be
fetched again.

Each project uses its own copy. Changes to the original dependency do not
affect that copy; the project author must update it to include a fix or a
new version.

Vendored source is not added to the module path. It is imported by a relative
path, for example:

````
(import ./deps/greetings)
````

The ``:vendored`` key in the info file contains a vector of source
declarations. ``wattle deps vendor`` copies the specified files into the
project, replacing files that already exist. Each declaration is a map with
these keys:

- ``:name``:
  A non-empty string identifying the source. Every declaration in
  ``:vendored`` must have a different name.

- ``:url``:
  A source directory, tarball URL ending in ``.tar`` or ``.tar.gz``, or Git
  repository, as for ``wattle apps install``.

- ``:ref``:
  An optional branch or tag for a Git repository. Without it, each invocation
  uses HEAD of the default branch.

- ``:prefix``:
  The destination directory, relative to the project. The default is the
  current directory.

- ``:paths``:
  An optional vector of paths to copy. Each entry is a string or a vector
  of two strings. A string specifies the same path in the source and under
  ``:prefix``. A pair specifies the source path followed by the destination
  path under ``:prefix``. Without ``:paths``, the whole source is copied.

Paths cannot be absolute or contain ``..``. All entries are checked before
any source is fetched. Vendoring records no source revision. The committed
copies determine the version used by the project.

A vendored source needs no ``info.edn`` and no library declaration. ``:for``
does not affect vendoring. For installation of a source executable, include
its vendored files in the executable's ``:files``.

REMOTE LIBRARIES
================

A *remote library* is a library fetched from another project. Its declaration
is a map in ``:libs`` with ``:type :remote``. The ``:url`` key specifies a Git
repository or a tarball URL ending in ``.tar`` or ``.tar.gz``. The ``:hash``
key specifies the exact source revision to use.

Git repositories
----------------

For a Git repository, ``:hash`` is a commit hash of 40 lowercase hexadecimal
digits, or 64 for a SHA-256 repository. Fetching first requests that commit
alone at depth 1. If the server does not allow a fetch by hash, Wattle clones
the repository instead. Fetching fails if the commit cannot be found.

The optional ``:ref`` specifies a branch or tag for the clone. It is needed
when the default branch does not reach the pinned commit. Otherwise it only
labels the commit; it does not select a different revision.

Tarballs
--------

For a tarball, ``:hash`` is ``sha256:`` followed by 64 lowercase hexadecimal
digits. This is the tree hash of the extracted files, as application
installation records it under ``:rev``, rather than a hash of the archive.
A tarball declaration cannot have ``:ref``.

Fetching
--------

If ``:hash`` is missing, fetching reports an error with the hash of the
fetched source. Add that hash to the declaration to pin the revision.
A library is fetched again only when its ``:url`` or ``:hash`` changes.

``wattle build lib`` fetches and places remote libraries on the module path.
``wattle build exe`` also fetches and places those associated with the native
executables it builds. Running a script does not fetch libraries.

The fetched source and its revision record are saved in
`.wattle/.remote/<name>/`. A source library is copied to
`.wattle/<name>.wattle` or `.wattle/<name>/`, depending on whether its root is
a file or directory. A native library is built in `zig-out/lib/`. Programs
import the library by the name in its declaration:

````
(import greetings)
````

A remote library requires a matching library declaration in the source
project. Source without an info file must be vendored instead. The ``:for``
of the local declaration associates the library with the project's
executables. Installation fetches associated remote libraries separately;
see `wattle-applications(7)`.

EXAMPLES
========

Vendor selected files into `deps/greetings/`:

````
{:vendored [{:name "greetings"
             :url "https://example.org/greetings.git"
             :ref "v1.0.0"
             :prefix "deps/greetings"
             :paths [["src/init.wattle" "init.wattle"] "LICENSE"]}]}
````

Copy the source:

````
$ wattle deps vendor
````

Declare a remote library for a native executable:

````
{:exes [{:type :native :name "hello" :entry "main.wattle"}]
 :libs [{:type :remote
         :name "greetings"
         :url "https://example.org/greetings.git"
         :hash "0123456789abcdef0123456789abcdef01234567"
         :for ["hello"]}]}
````

The hash must identify a commit in the source repository. A branch or tag in
``:ref`` does not replace it.

